{"baseline":{"timeout_pct":0,"avg_sec":160.04,"success_pct":0.995},"task":"[Автозадача из бэклога] [cc reliability ROOT — segfault, dedicated STA COM thread] In layers/uia.py add a dedicated long-lived STA worker thread that owns COM for the whole process. At module load start ONE daemon threading.Thread that calls comtypes.CoInitializeEx(_COINIT_APARTMENTTHREADED) ONCE, then loops servicing a queue.Queue of (callable, future) work items. Add _run_on_uia_apartment(fn, timeout=None) that submits fn and blocks on the future. Route ALL COM-touching public entry points through it: _get_uia_singleton, find_element, set_value, get_value, invoke_element, expand_element, press_key (hwnd path), the UIA tree walk, and release_com_elements. Remove the _com_init_done assumption that any thread may CoInitialize — ONLY the apartment thread calls CoInitializeEx. Register an atexit handler that releases cached COM refs ON the apartment thread. ROOT (high-confidence diagnosis): COM apartment was created on a throwaway per-step pool thread but the singleton + cached _com_element proxies are used and released from OTHER threads -\u003e cross-apartment STA access violation -\u003e intermittent segfault (exit 139, ~2/3 runs). Making every COM op happen on the single STA thread eliminates both the cross-thread-use crash and the release/GC-on-main-thread crash. Add tests/test_uia.py: same _uia_singleton id returned when called from N different threads; release_com_elements from a non-apartment thread runs the null-out ON the apartment thread (thread-id capture stub); a \u003e=20-iteration find_element+set_value+release loop across distinct threads with zero crash and no RPC_E_WRONG_THREAD.","commit":"688ca961ef84ad5ccb315b4ca1ac4058e8cdde20","type":"hypothesis","ts":"2026-06-16T10:19:01.2084645Z"}
